{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786797072,"nonce":"Fa+56TnbTpZDljFgChilbr5tkoM=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABM4WvLg","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/102","origin":"github"},"title":"Vector-valued mortar ties","message":"## What\n\nNon-conforming mortar ties (`u(A) - u(B)` across a `conforming=False` interface) are **scalar-only**; on a vector field the tie raises. This is the remaining sibling of the contact work: one-sided contact, AL exactness, and the assembled gap tangent all landed (2026-08-15), so the vector tie is now the last gap in the contact/mortar row of the library comparison.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nIt blocks the \"tie vs contact on one interface\" elasticity tutorial: two bodies joined by a tie on one part of the interface and by unilateral contact on another — the canonical demonstrator that the two mechanisms are the same pairing machinery with different constitutive closures. The gap machinery already handles vector fields (`u.gap` requires `value_shape=(dim,)`), and the mortar projection tables (`ids/w`) are component-agnostic, so the plumbing gap is in the tie *lowering*, not the pairing.\n\n## Acceptance sketch\n\n- A vector tie on the bonded stack reproduces the single-bar oracle exactly as the scalar tie does on scalar problems (the existing patch-test oracles generalized).\n- The tie and a stiff bonded penalty converge to each other.\n- Loud refusal removed only when both are green.","files":null}]}